# Compose with {{SYSTEM_NAME}}

These examples use the twenty required V4 contracts and public package imports.
They also work with a different V4 system after changing the package import.
Optional capabilities must be checked in the installed `./manifest` first.
Import `{{PACKAGE_NAME}}/styles.css` once in the application entry point.
For Tailwind v4, use the import order shown in [README.md](./README.md).

## A content page

The product owns content, routes, actions, and page composition. The package
owns typography, surfaces, component spacing, variants, and interaction states.

```tsx
import {
  Button,
  Card,
  Container,
  Heading,
  Link,
  Stack,
  Text,
} from "{{PACKAGE_NAME}}";

export function Overview({ onCreate }: { onCreate: () => void }) {
  return (
    <Container as="main">
      <Stack>
        <Heading level={1}>Your workspace</Heading>
        <Text>Keep the next step visible to everyone on the team.</Text>
        <Card>
          <Card.Header>
            <Card.Title>Start a project</Card.Title>
            <Card.Description>Give your work a shared home.</Card.Description>
          </Card.Header>
          <Card.Content>
            <Stack direction="horizontal" wrap>
              <Button onClick={onCreate}>Create project</Button>
              <Link href="/projects">Browse projects</Link>
            </Stack>
          </Card.Content>
        </Card>
      </Stack>
    </Container>
  );
}
```

`Stack` uses `direction` and `wrap`; it has no `variant` prop. Use semantic
HTML for product regions and layout utilities for product placement. Choose
token names from `prism-ds tokens`, rather than inventing utilities or applying
visual classes to package components.

## An interactive form

In Next.js App Router this module needs `"use client"`. Supply the real product
action through `onSave`; validation and request state remain in the product.

```tsx
"use client";

import * as React from "react";
import { Button, FormField, Input, Select, Stack, Text } from "{{PACKAGE_NAME}}";

export function InviteForm({
  onSave,
}: {
  onSave: (input: { email: string; role: string }) => Promise<void>;
}) {
  const id = React.useId();
  const emailRef = React.useRef<HTMLInputElement>(null);
  const [email, setEmail] = React.useState("");
  const [role, setRole] = React.useState("member");
  const [error, setError] = React.useState<string>();
  const [requestError, setRequestError] = React.useState<string>();
  const [pending, setPending] = React.useState(false);

  async function submit(event: React.FormEvent<HTMLFormElement>) {
    event.preventDefault();
    if (pending) return;
    setRequestError(undefined);
    if (!email.trim() || !emailRef.current?.validity.valid) {
      setError("Enter a valid email address.");
      emailRef.current?.focus();
      return;
    }
    setError(undefined);
    setPending(true);
    try {
      await onSave({ email: email.trim(), role });
    } catch {
      setRequestError("The invitation could not be sent. Please try again.");
    } finally {
      setPending(false);
    }
  }

  return (
    <form onSubmit={submit} noValidate>
      <Stack>
        <FormField id={`${id}-email`} required invalid={Boolean(error)} disabled={pending}>
          <FormField.Label>Email address</FormField.Label>
          <FormField.Control asChild>
            <Input
              ref={emailRef}
              type="email"
              required
              autoComplete="email"
              value={email}
              aria-describedby={`${id}-privacy`}
              onChange={(event) => setEmail(event.target.value)}
            />
          </FormField.Control>
          <FormField.Description>Use the address your teammate works with.</FormField.Description>
          {error && <FormField.Error>{error}</FormField.Error>}
        </FormField>
        <Text id={`${id}-privacy`}>This address is used only for this invitation.</Text>
        <Select value={role} onValueChange={setRole} disabled={pending}>
          <FormField id={`${id}-role`} disabled={pending}>
            <FormField.Label>Role</FormField.Label>
            <FormField.Control asChild>
              <Select.Trigger>
                <Select.Value />
              </Select.Trigger>
            </FormField.Control>
            <FormField.Description>
              Choose the permissions for this workspace.
            </FormField.Description>
          </FormField>
          <Select.Content>
            <Select.Item value="member">Member</Select.Item>
            <Select.Item value="viewer">Viewer</Select.Item>
          </Select.Content>
        </Select>
        {requestError && <Text role="alert">{requestError}</Text>}
        <Button type="submit" loading={pending}>
          Send invitation
        </Button>
      </Stack>
    </form>
  );
}
```

`FormField.Control asChild` forwards the field id and state to its one child,
preserves the child's ref and handlers, and combines existing ARIA descriptions
with the mounted help/error parts. Client registration updates those part
references after hydration. The default `Control` renders a `div` wrapper;
use `asChild` to wire the actual focusable control. For `Select`, wire the
`Trigger`, and set root `Select.disabled` for its popup behavior too.

`FormField.required` exposes `aria-required`. Set native `Input.required` when
native validity is needed. `noValidate` in this example lets the product choose
when to display the error and focus the preserved input ref. Root `invalid`
sets the input's invalid appearance without an additional system-specific prop.

## Verify the consumer

Run `prism-ds check --cwd . --css <entry.css>` to check configuration, component
usage, and the selected CSS entry, or `prism-ds check-usage --cwd .` for usage
alone. These checks are read-only. They catch supported syntax violations, not
every visual override: semantic utility classes and unrelated stylesheets still
need review. Inspect keyboard access, focus, errors, disabled state, and responsive
composition in the real product.
